  wModel: { title: 'the standard, as issued', lines: [
    '**BRANCH SERVICE STANDARD 1 · counter waiting · issued 10 April 2026 · supersedes the standard of January 2024**',
    '**The standard.** **No customer will wait longer than twenty minutes to be served at a counter.**',
    '**Measurement.** From the time printed on the customer\'s ticket to the moment a cashier begins their transaction. **Not from when they join the queue, and not from when they reach the front. This is stated here because it was the subject of disagreement at four of the last six operations meetings.**',
    '**Who tests it, and when.** The cashier, at the counter, at the moment a ticket reaches twenty minutes. **No calculation is required of anybody and nothing is referred to another building.**',
    '**What happens on a breach.** **At twenty minutes the counter supervisor is called** and will either **(a)** open a second position, or **(b)** tell the waiting customer, in person, how long the wait will be. **One of the two must happen; neither is optional, and "nothing could be done" is not a third option.** The supervisor records which, on the daily sheet, with the ticket number.',
    '**Why twenty and not ten.** The January 2024 standard was ten minutes. **It was breached on every salary Thursday — about twenty-four days a year — and in those two years no breach ever produced an action.** **A standard breached two dozen times a year is not a standard, and the cost is not the queue: it is that the counter learns our standards are decorative, and applies that to the next one we write.** **Twenty minutes is achievable on every day of the year, including salary Thursdays, and a breach of it will mean something.**' ] },
